several young people from the west las  vegas community spoke directly with law  enforcement today in onnoya rights  workshop this happened at doolittle  community center 8 news now reporter  victoria saha was there she joins us  live in the newsroom tonight with a look  at how these events help build trust on  both sides victoria  hey sasha the teens and law enforcement  i spoke with say events like this helps  them understand both perspectives if  they were to be approached by police and  know the right steps to take this  program kicked off when trayon martin  was killed in florida in 2012. you may  remember martin was 17 when he was  walking home and was shot and killed by  a neighborhood watch volunteer george  zimmerman zimmerman claimed it was  self-defense although martin was  unharmed  since then there have been numerous  police encounters with people of color  that have made headlines today's  workshop was led by attorneys and law  enforcement to show the community of  color what their rights are when  approached by police and what not to do  to escalate a situation it's just making  them feel comfortable and letting the  youth know that hey we're here to  protect and serve you know  again i think the issue at hand is it's  a lot of  a lot of things they see on youtube a  lot of things they see on social media  officer hunt grew up in the northwest  las vegas area he says he saw negative  interactions with law enforcement  growing up which made him want to become  an officer to change that  a key takeaway from this event is to let  youth know if they were to encounter  police then they just need to stay calm  and comply running away is not the  option reporting live from the newsroom  victoria saha 8 news now
 

